/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  filter03_drum_area_medium_tabulatedWash

  A ROTARY VACUUM DRUM SIZED BY ITS AREA, WITH A MEDIUM RESISTANCE AND A
  TABULATED WASH.  filter01's slurry and cake (Geankoplis, Hersel & Lepek
  5th ed., Example 24.2-4: 0.778 kg/s, c_x = 0.191, m = 2.0, alpha =
  4.37e9 dp^0.3, 67 kPa, 33 % submergence), run through the three drum
  branches filter01 does not reach:
    * the AREA is declared (6.0 m2) and the cycle time is the result --
      Geankoplis eq. 24.2-25 with V = Q_f t_c, solved for t_c;
    * the medium carries a resistance, R_m = 1.0e10 1/m, so Ruth's B V term
      (eq. 24.2-17) is not zero: dropping it changes this case's answer;
    * the cake is WASHED on the drum by 0.2 kg/s of water at the final
      filtration rate (eq. 24.2-19), with the solute left in the cake read
      off a TABULATED wash test, interpolated linearly in (N, ln R) -- the
      coordinates of Perry's log R vs N correlation (9th ed., eq. 18-71).

  HELD TO (the hand-written rows at the top of `expected`): t_c, the
  filtration time, K_p, B, the wash time, the wash ratio N and the solute
  remaining R, recomputed by an independent script
  (verification/drumClosedForm.py, Python standard library, written from
  Geankoplis's equations and the declared wash table, not from the C++),
  with the water's viscosity and density the package publishes as its
  inputs.  The formation-time closure (eq. 24.2-17 against f t_c) is an
  identity the unit itself prints.

  WHAT ITS NUMBERS ARE NOT: a validation, and not Geankoplis's example.
  The area, R_m, the wash flow and the wash test are ILLUSTRATIVE values
  chosen for this case; no book prints this drum.  The liquor is pure
  water (the case's sealed records carry no solute), so the wash's
  componentwise displacement is not visible here: the published R is the
  wash law's answer, and the mass balance closes on water alone.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
application   choupoSolve;
description   "A rotary vacuum drum with a declared area (cycle time the result), a medium resistance R_m = 1e10 1/m and a cake wash read off a tabulated wash test, on Geankoplis Example 24.2-4's CaCO3 slurry: illustrative values, held to an independent recomputation of Ruth's law.";
verbosity     3;
citations
(
    "Geankoplis, C.J.; Hersel, A.A.; Lepek, D.H. Transport Processes and Separation Process Principles, 5th ed. Prentice Hall (year not read on the page). Section 24.2, eqs. 24.2-10 to 24.2-26, Example 24.2-4, pp. 1078-1090."
    "Perry's Chemical Engineers' Handbook, 9th ed. McGraw-Hill. Section 18, wash ratio and eq. 18-71, p. 18-76 (the log R vs N coordinates of a wash test; no constants)."
);
